{"id":"46570e8a-6fd1-4158-92d3-03a389519250","article_key":"doi:10.1016/j.cell.2026.10.023","title":"Spatial transcriptomics reveals molecular organization of human and nonhuman primate basal ganglia","title_zh":"空间转录组揭示人类和非人灵长类动物基底神经节的分子组织","doi":"10.1016/j.cell.2026.10.023","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2026.10.023","abstract":"The basal ganglia (BG) are interconnected subcortical nuclei with a complex topographical organization that orchestrate goal-directed behaviors and are implicated in neurodegenerative movement disorders. We generated a cellular-resolution spatial transcriptomics atlas of the BG in human, rhesus macaque, and common marmoset, sampling over one million cells in each species. By integrating spatial data with a cross-species consensus single-nucleus RNA sequencing (snRNA-seq) cell-type taxonomy, this atlas reveals principles of molecular organization within and across structures. Extensive spatial sampling across the rostral-caudal axis illuminates complex, yet highly stereotyped, multi-axis gene expression gradients superimposed onto discrete compartments of the striatum. Additionally, we identify core-shell organization of the internal globus pallidus. This unified, cross-species spatial transcriptomics atlas will be a foundational resource for characterizing the molecular and functional organization of the BG and their roles in health and disease.","abstract_zh":"基底神经节（BG）是相互连接的皮质下核团，具有复杂的地形组织，协调目标导向行为，并与神经退行性运动障碍相关。我们生成了人类、恒河猴和普通黑白卷尾猴BG的细胞分辨率空间转录组图谱，每种物种采样超过百万个细胞。通过将空间数据与跨物种共识单核RNA测序（snRNA-seq）细胞类型分类体系相结合，该图谱揭示了结构内部和跨物种的分子组织原理。沿前-后轴的广泛空间采样阐明了叠加于纹状体离散区室上的复杂且高度刻板的多轴基因表达梯度。此外，我们发现内侧苍白球具有核-壳组织。这个统一的跨物种空间转录组图谱将成为表征基底神经节分子和功能组织及其在健康和疾病中的作用的基础资源。","snippet":"The basal ganglia (BG) are interconnected subcortical nuclei with a complex topographical organization that orchestrate goal-directed behaviors and are implicated in neurodegenerative movement disorders. We generated a cellular-resolution spatial transcriptomics atlas of the BG in human, rhesus macaque, and common marmoset, sampling over one million cells in each species. By integrating spatial data with a cross-spe…","translated_at":"2026-11-14T08:11:26.352478Z","translation_model":"nvidia/nemotron-3.5-super-vl-120b-a12b","source_category":"doi","article_type":"journal-article","volume":"189","issue":"23","published_at":"2026-11-01T00:00:00","acquired_at":"2026-11-12T23:34:37Z","display_date":"2026-11-12T23:34:37Z","display_date_source":"acquired","online_published_at":null,"print_published_at":"2026-11-01T00:00:00","first_author":"Madeleine N. Hewitt","authors_text":"Madeleine N. Hewitt, Meghan A. Turner, Nelson Johansen, Delissa A. McMillen, Shu Dan, Michael DeBerardine, Augustin Ruiz, Mike Huang, Jacob Quon, Yuanyuan Fu, Inkar Kapen, Stuard Barta, Naomi Martin, Nasmil Valera Cuevas, Paul Olsen, Josh Nagra, Jazmin Campos, Marshall M. VanNess, Shea Ransford, Zoe Juneau, Sam Hastings, Lindsey Ching, Michael Kunst, Soumyadeep Basu, Thomas Höllt, Chang Li, Boudewijn Lelieveldt, Faraz Yazdani, Qiangge Zhang, Kirsten Levandowski, Guoping Feng, Burke Q. Rosen, Matthew F. Glasser, Takuya Hayashi, Aaron D. Garcia, Omar Kana, Zoe M. Maltzer, Luke Campagnola, Tim Jarsky, Lauren Kruse, Winrich Freiwald, C. Dirk Keene, David C. Van Essen, Jeanelle Ariza, Jack Waters, Fenna M. Krienen, Trygve E. Bakken, Rebecca D. Hodge, Lydia Ng, Hongkui Zeng, Ed S. Lein, Jennie L. Close, Brian Long, Stephanie C. Seeman","authors":[{"position":0,"given_name":"Madeleine N.","family_name":"Hewitt","full_name":"Madeleine N. Hewitt","affiliation":null},{"position":1,"given_name":"Meghan A.","family_name":"Turner","full_name":"Meghan A. Turner","affiliation":null},{"position":2,"given_name":"Nelson","family_name":"Johansen","full_name":"Nelson Johansen","affiliation":null},{"position":3,"given_name":"Delissa A.","family_name":"McMillen","full_name":"Delissa A. McMillen","affiliation":null},{"position":4,"given_name":"Shu","family_name":"Dan","full_name":"Shu Dan","affiliation":n